# Abstraction

## What is Abstraction

Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features.



## Real-Life Example

* Car → you drive without knowing engine internals
* ATM → you withdraw money without knowing backend logic



##  How to Achieve Abstraction in Java

1. Abstract Class
2. Interface



# Abstract Class

##  What it is

A class declared with `abstract` keyword.

* Can have abstract methods (no body)
* Can have normal methods (with body)



## Syntax

abstract class ClassName {
    abstract void methodName(); // no body
}


## Example

abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound(); // abstract method

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}



## Implementation

class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}



## Key Points (Abstract Class)

* Cannot create object directly
* Can have constructors
* Can have instance variables
* Supports inheritance



### Use Abstract Class:

* When classes share common behavior
* When you need constructors or state


##  Advantages

* Hides complexity
* Improves security
* Makes code flexible
* Supports loose coupling


## Best Practices

* Use meaningful abstraction
* Don’t over-abstract simple logic
* Prefer interface for flexibility
* Use abstract class for shared code
